</style>

<!-- SERVICE CARDS -->
<section style="padding:0 24px 80px;background:#F1ECE2;">
  <div style="max-width:1100px;margin:0 auto;">
    <div class="svc-grid" style="display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(4,1fr);gap:20px;">
      <a href="/wall-removal-engineering/" style="display:flex;flex-direction:column;background:#fff;border:1px solid #ECE5D8;border-radius:12px;padding:30px 26px;text-decoration:none;box-shadow:0 2px 8px rgba(14,77,110,0.06);">
        <h3 style="font-family:'Figtree',sans-serif;font-size:20px;font-weight:800;color:#15191E;margin:0 0 10px;">Wall Removal & Beam Design</h3>
        <p style="font-size:14.5px;line-height:1.7;color:#555;margin:0 0 18px;flex:1;">The engineer works out what the wall carries and sizes the steel or LVL beam, posts and pad footings that take its place. You get the drawing and certificate your building surveyor needs, and a registered builder does the work.</p>
        <span style="font-size:13px;font-weight:700;color:#1F5E8C;letter-spacing:0.04em;text-transform:uppercase;">Learn More &#8594;</span>
      </a>
      <a href="/structural-inspections/" style="display:flex;flex-direction:column;background:#fff;border:1px solid #ECE5D8;border-radius:12px;padding:30px 26px;text-decoration:none;box-shadow:0 2px 8px rgba(14,77,110,0.06);">
        <h3 style="font-family:'Figtree',sans-serif;font-size:20px;font-weight:800;color:#15191E;margin:0 0 10px;">Crack & Structural Inspection Reports</h3>
        <p style="font-size:14.5px;line-height:1.7;color:#555;margin:0 0 18px;flex:1;">An engineer looks at the cracks, the walls, the slab and footings and tells you in writing what they think is going on. It's a different product from a pre-purchase building inspection, and worth knowing which one you're paying for.</p>
        <span style="font-size:13px;font-weight:700;color:#1F5E8C;letter-spacing:0.04em;text-transform:uppercase;">Learn More &#8594;</span>
      </a>
      <a href="/extension-engineering/" style="display:flex;flex-direction:column;background:#fff;border:1px solid #ECE5D8;border-radius:12px;padding:30px 26px;text-decoration:none;box-shadow:0 2px 8px rgba(14,77,110,0.06);">
        <h3 style="font-family:'Figtree',sans-serif;font-size:20px;font-weight:800;color:#15191E;margin:0 0 10px;">Extension & Second-Storey Engineering</h3>
        <p style="font-size:14.5px;line-height:1.7;color:#555;margin:0 0 18px;flex:1;">Any change to floor area or height needs a building permit, and the builder or building surveyor will usually want engineering to go with it. The engineer designs the structural parts, such as beams, footings and slabs, so the plans can go in.</p>
        <span style="font-size:13px;font-weight:700;color:#1F5E8C;letter-spacing:0.04em;text-transform:uppercase;">Learn More &#8594;</span>
      </a>
      <a href="/patio-shed-certificates/" style="display:flex;flex-direction:column;background:#fff;border:1px solid #ECE5D8;border-radius:12px;padding:30px 26px;text-decoration:none;box-shadow:0 2px 8px rgba(14,77,110,0.06);">
        <h3 style="font-family:'Figtree',sans-serif;font-size:20px;font-weight:800;color:#15191E;margin:0 0 10px;">Patio, Carport & Shed Certificates</h3>
        <p style="font-size:14.5px;line-height:1.7;color:#555;margin:0 0 18px;flex:1;">A small pergola or a garden shed can be exempt, but a roofed patio isn't a pergola under WA rules. If yours needs a permit, the engineer provides site-specific drawings and a signed certificate for the application.</p>
        <span style="font-size:13px;font-weight:700;color:#1F5E8C;letter-spacing:0.04em;text-transform:uppercase;">Learn More &#8594;</span>
      </a>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
<style>
  @media (max-width: 1000px) { .svc-grid { grid-template-columns: repeat(2,1fr) !important; } }
  @media (max-width: 600px) { .svc-grid { grid-template-columns: 1fr !important; } }
