<!-- AREAS -->
<section style="padding:76px 24px 60px;background:#fff;">
  <div style="max-width:860px;margin:0 auto;text-align:center;">
    <span class="kicker">Service Areas</span>
    <h2 class="section-heading" style="font-size:clamp(24px,3vw,36px);margin:0 0 16px;">Serving Perth</h2>
    <div class="rich" style="text-align:left;"><p>The site covers Greater Perth, the Perth Hills and Peel: <a href="/structural-engineer-joondalup-wanneroo/">Joondalup and Wanneroo</a>, <a href="/structural-engineer-western-suburbs-fremantle/">the western suburbs and Fremantle</a>, <a href="/structural-engineer-perth-hills-eastern-suburbs/">the Perth Hills and eastern suburbs</a>, <a href="/structural-engineer-cockburn-melville-canning/">Cockburn, Melville and Canning</a>, <a href="/structural-engineer-rockingham-kwinana/">Rockingham and Kwinana</a> and <a href="/structural-engineer-mandurah-peel/">Mandurah and Peel</a>. The ground isn't the same everywhere. Much of central Perth sits on Bassendean Sand, limestone runs along the coast, and the Guildford Formation clay outcrops across much of the eastern region, which is one reason <a href="/blog/why-perth-houses-crack/">Perth houses crack</a> for different reasons in different places.</p></div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- FAQ -->
<section style="padding:72px 24px;background:#F1ECE2;">
  <div style="max-width:860px;margin:0 auto;">
    <span class="kicker">FAQ</span>
    <h2 class="section-heading" style="font-size:clamp(24px,3.5vw,36px);margin:0 0 32px;">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
    <div style="display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:16px;">
      <div style="background:#fff;border-radius:14px;padding:24px 28px;box-shadow:0 3px 12px rgba(10,61,92,0.06);">
        <h3 style="font-family:'Figtree',sans-serif;font-size:17px;font-weight:800;color:#15191E;margin:0 0 10px;">How do I know if a wall is load-bearing?</h3>
        <p style="font-size:15px;line-height:1.75;color:#5A6B76;margin:0;">There are clues. In a double-brick house some internal walls usually carry the ceiling and roof, and anything in the roof space that lands on the wall is a strong hint. None of that tells you a wall is safe to remove, though. Only an engineer who has looked at the roof, the ceiling and what sits above can say what the wall carries and what beam replaces it, and a registered builder does the actual removal.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="background:#fff;border-radius:14px;padding:24px 28px;box-shadow:0 3px 12px rgba(10,61,92,0.06);">
        <h3 style="font-family:'Figtree',sans-serif;font-size:17px;font-weight:800;color:#15191E;margin:0 0 10px;">How much does a structural engineer cost in Perth, and how is the job scoped?</h3>
        <p style="font-size:15px;line-height:1.75;color:#5A6B76;margin:0;">Perth engineering firms generally quote per job rather than publishing a price list, because the fee depends on the house, the structure and what the council needs. The engineer looks at your photos or plans first, decides whether a site visit is needed, and then puts the scope and fee in a written quote. Ask whether that fee is incl. or ex GST. Government charges are separate: a certified building permit is 0.19% of the value of the work and an uncertified one 0.32%, both with a $121.00 minimum, and the value is calculated including GST.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="background:#fff;border-radius:14px;padding:24px 28px;box-shadow:0 3px 12px rgba(10,61,92,0.06);">
        <h3 style="font-family:'Figtree',sans-serif;font-size:17px;font-weight:800;color:#15191E;margin:0 0 10px;">How quickly can an engineer look at my job, and how long does the permit take?</h3>
        <p style="font-size:15px;line-height:1.75;color:#5A6B76;margin:0;">Availability and turnaround vary with the engineer's workload, so ask for both and get them in the written proposal. If a crack is getting wider, or doors and windows have started sticking, say so on the first call. Once the engineering is done, the permit authority has 10 business days to decide a certified application and 25 business days for an uncertified one, and the clock stops if it asks you for more information.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="background:#fff;border-radius:14px;padding:24px 28px;box-shadow:0 3px 12px rgba(10,61,92,0.06);">
        <h3 style="font-family:'Figtree',sans-serif;font-size:17px;font-weight:800;color:#15191E;margin:0 0 10px;">Is the crack in my wall serious?</h3>
        <p style="font-size:15px;line-height:1.75;color:#5A6B76;margin:0;">CSIRO grades crack damage by width: under 0.1 mm is negligible, under 1 mm very slight, under 5 mm slight, 5 to 15 mm moderate and 15 to 25 mm severe. It says most cracking in masonry walls is a cosmetic nuisance, and also that monitoring cracks should always be taken seriously. Width is only part of the story, and nobody can tell you what your crack means without seeing it, which is what an engineer's inspection is for.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="background:#fff;border-radius:14px;padding:24px 28px;box-shadow:0 3px 12px rgba(10,61,92,0.06);">
        <h3 style="font-family:'Figtree',sans-serif;font-size:17px;font-weight:800;color:#15191E;margin:0 0 10px;">Do I need an engineer for a patio, carport or shed?</h3>
        <p style="font-size:15px;line-height:1.75;color:#5A6B76;margin:0;">Not always. A freestanding shed of 10 m2 or less and no more than 2.4 m high can be exempt from a permit, as can an unroofed pergola up to 20 m2, provided you're not in wind region C or D. A roofed patio isn't a pergola under WA rules, so it usually needs a permit, and the council or building surveyor will often ask for an engineer's certificate (sometimes called a certificate of structural adequacy or sufficiency).</p>
      </div>
      <div style="background:#fff;border-radius:14px;padding:24px 28px;box-shadow:0 3px 12px rgba(10,61,92,0.06);">
        <h3 style="font-family:'Figtree',sans-serif;font-size:17px;font-weight:800;color:#15191E;margin:0 0 10px;">Is a building inspection the same as a structural engineer's report?</h3>
        <p style="font-size:15px;line-height:1.75;color:#5A6B76;margin:0;">No. Many reports sold in Perth as structural inspections are AS 4349.1 pre-purchase reports written by building inspectors, and some of those firms say plainly that they don't provide engineering. An engineer's report looks at the walls, roof structure, floors, slab and footings and gives an engineering opinion on what's causing the problem. If you're buying, or in a dispute with a builder or insurer, check which one you're paying for before you book.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

